 15 4.10.2 Additional Model Requirements .................................................................................... 15 5 Key Metrics/Test Cases for Model Evaluation ................................................................................. 17 6 Challenges and Considerations ........................................................................................................ 21 6.1 Computational Complexity ......................................................................................................... 21 6.2 Model Fidelity vs. Practicality .................................................................................................... 21 6.3 Trade-Off Decisions .................................................................................................................... 21 7 Conclusion .......................................................................................................................................... 23 References ................................................................................................................................................. 24 Appendix .................................................................................................................................................... 28 v This report is available at no cost from the National Laboratory of the Rockies (NLR) at www.nlr.gov/publications. List of Figures Figure 1. Overview of data center power system architectures across various entities. ............................... 5 Figure 2. ITIC curve ..................................................................................................................................... 6 List of Tables Table 1. Key Metrics and Test Cases for Model Evaluation ...................................................................... 17 Table A-1. Test Case Description and Technical Relevance for User Reference ....................................... 28 vi This report is available at no cost from the National Laboratory of the Rockies (NLR) at www.nlr.gov/publications. Introduction There is tremendous activity in data center interconnection requests throughout the United States, with some utilities having queues exceeding their total current operational capacity by a few multiples. This is fueled by a significant demand for data center computing capacity for artificial intelligence (AI) applications. A recent report from Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory (Shehabi et al. 2024) projects data center energy consumption to be 6.7−12% of the total U.S. energy consumption by 2028, compared to 4.4% of the total U.S. energy consumption in 2025. The Long-Term Reliability Assessment report from the North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) (2024) identifies that most of the North American bulk power system faces significant resource adequacy challenges in the next 10 years due to surging energy demand, the retirement of thermal generators, and the time to completion of new power plant projects. In most locations, power grids have limited available capacity to support data center campuses ranging from several hundred megawatts to the gigawatt scale (or, as NVIDIA refers to them, AI factories). To address these capacity constraints in the near",
